# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
descartes-discourse-1.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: descartes-discourse-1.logic.r2 and descartes-discourse-1.logic.r2.2 have nothing from it; descartes-discourse-1.logic.r2.3 has "¬vigor_enough ∧ apply"; they differ when only "to be possessed of a vigorous mind is enough" is true, which the first allows
descartes-discourse-1.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps descartes-discourse-1.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "to be possessed of a vigorous mind is enough" is true and "the prime requisite is rightly to apply the mind" is false. Is S3, "For to be possessed of a vigorous mind is not enough; the prime requisite is rightly to apply it.", true then?
descartes-discourse-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: descartes-discourse-1.logic.r2 has "content ∧ ¬all_mistaken → equal" and "equal → ¬diversity_unequal" and "great_aberr ∧ slow_progress → ¬vigor_enough ∧ apply" and "apply ∧ ¬diversity_unequal → diversity_method"; descartes-discourse-1.logic.r2.2 has "content ∧ ¬all_mistaken → equal" and "great_aberr ∧ slow_progress → ¬vigor_enough ∧ apply" and "equal → ¬diversity_unequal" and "¬vigor_enough ∧ apply → diversity_method"; they differ when only "every one thinks himself so abundantly provided with good sense that even those hardest to satisfy do not desire a larger measure of it than they already possess", "in this conviction all are mistaken", "the diversity of our opinions arises from some being endowed with a larger share of reason than others" and "the prime requisite is rightly to apply the mind" are true, which the first allows; descartes-discourse-1.logic.r2.3 has "content ∧ ¬all_mistaken → equal" and "equal → ¬diversity_unequal" and "diversity_unequal ∨ diversity_method"
descartes-discourse-1.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: descartes-discourse-1.logic.r2 has "diversity_method"; descartes-discourse-1.logic.r2.2 and descartes-discourse-1.logic.r2.3 have "¬diversity_unequal ∧ diversity_method"; they differ when only "the diversity of our opinions arises from some being endowed with a larger share of reason than others" and "the diversity of our opinions arises from our conducting our thoughts along different ways and not fixing our attention on the same objects" are true, which the first allows
descartes-discourse-1.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps descartes-discourse-1.logic.r2.2 and descartes-discourse-1.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the diversity of our opinions arises from some being endowed with a larger share of reason than others" and "the diversity of our opinions arises from our conducting our thoughts along different ways and not fixing our attention on the same objects" are true. Is the conclusion, "And in this it is not likely that all are mistaken the conviction is rather to be held as testifying that the power of judging aright and of distinguishing truth from error, which is properly what is called good sense or reason, is by nature equal in all men; and that the diversity of our opinions, consequently, does not arise from some being endowed with a larger share of reason than others, but solely from this, that we conduct our thoughts along different ways, and do not fix our attention on the same objects.", true then?
